            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Very strange indeed.\r\nCouple of thoughts:\r\n1) That kind of statusline (default builtin) usually appears when some Lua code has gone wrong.\r\n2) Does this happen when you press dot to repeat it instead of a new `df)`?\r\n3) Do you have any other plugin installed which might alter the `df)`? One way to check is to type `omap f` in Command line. It should give the `MiniJump` function.\r\n4) The usual way to debug this is a good old \"half the config and try reproduce\". If you can reproduce this with only 'mini.jump', then it is a problem with it only. If not - then it is some kind of interference issue with other plugin. Either way, if you can achieve stable reproducible example of this, I'd be super curious to see it.\r\n5) Usual questions: which Neovim version you are using and is config public?",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Very strange indeed.<br>\nCouple of thoughts:</p>\n<ol dir=\"auto\">\n<li>That kind of statusline (default builtin) usually appears when some Lua code has gone wrong.</li>\n<li>Does this happen when you press dot to repeat it instead of a new <code class=\"notranslate\">df)</code>?</li>\n<li>Do you have any other plugin installed which might alter the <code class=\"notranslate\">df)</code>? One way to check is to type <code class=\"notranslate\">omap f</code> in Command line. It should give the <code class=\"notranslate\">MiniJump</code> function.</li>\n<li>The usual way to debug this is a good old \"half the config and try reproduce\". If you can reproduce this with only 'mini.jump', then it is a problem with it only. If not - then it is some kind of interference issue with other plugin. Either way, if you can achieve stable reproducible example of this, I'd be super curious to see it.</li>\n<li>Usual questions: which Neovim version you are using and is config public?</li>\n</ol>",
            "bodyText": "Very strange indeed.\nCouple of thoughts:\n\nThat kind of statusline (default builtin) usually appears when some Lua code has gone wrong.\nDoes this happen when you press dot to repeat it instead of a new df)?\nDo you have any other plugin installed which might alter the df)? One way to check is to type omap f in Command line. It should give the MiniJump function.\nThe usual way to debug this is a good old \"half the config and try reproduce\". If you can reproduce this with only 'mini.jump', then it is a problem with it only. If not - then it is some kind of interference issue with other plugin. Either way, if you can achieve stable reproducible example of this, I'd be super curious to see it.\nUsual questions: which Neovim version you are using and is config public?",
